 Beans n sausage scraps.

This dish came about as a way to use up the scraps from making sausages. It has a few different starting points.

First starting point : 
Start brining 2 cups of dried pink means. This should be done overnight. So you'll need to start that yesterday. (2 cup beans, 2 qt water, 2 tbl salt)

Second starting point :
Making sausages. Julie brought home about a dozen or so, bone in, pork chops.

Meat is running around $10..$20+/lb these days. She found these chops at about $3/lb. She bought a LOT!

So I decided to make some sausages. This was the beginning of my chili spice sausages. I ended up with a lot of sausages, blew up a few, so there was about a pound of loose sausage meat. A huge mess, and a bunch of pork bones.

What to do? I decided to do a sausage pork n beans kinda' thing outta' all the scraps.

Third starting point :
Put all the bones into the Dutch oven. Cover in water, and start simmering them to make stock. These go for about 1 1/2 hours or so.

Fourth starting point :
Rinse the beans you started yesterday. put them in a pot, cover in water, add 2 tsp salt and get them boiling. Once they are boiling, cover and reduce the heat to low. Let the simmer for about 1 1/2 hours. You'll need to stir them now and then. Every about 25 min or so.

Fifth starting point :
Deseed and chop up..
1..2 Paseo peppers.
2..4 Jalapino peppers.
1 Sweet red pepper.
4 Little unknown red peppers Julie brought in from the back yard.
Chop up, in your favorite way..
1 Giant onion.
Set all these aside in a bowl.

About now the beans should be done cooking. Grab a colander or strainer and dump them into this letting the "bean water" drain away. Rinse them a few times. Toss them in a bowl and, for now, put them in the fridge to wait.

And also getting ready to go should be the bone broth. When the meat is falling off the bones, the broth is done. Ladle out all the bones and meat onto a large cutting board. Carefully pick the meat off the bones. Reserve the meat and toss the bones and cartilage that's left over. I ended up with about a pound of meat from the bones.

Wash up the bean pot and pour off the bone broth into it. Carful not to get any bone chips or other nasty things in there.

Ok Now you have all your bits ready for assembly.
Your veggies,
Your beans, 
Your bone broth,
Your left over meat,
Your sausage meat.

Assembly :
Onions and peppers into the dutch oven with some olive oil, and salt. Get them frying. Don't let the heat get too high. You want them kinda' caramelized NOT burnt! As they fry, if things get too hot? Ladle in some of your bone broth. Doing this low and slow can take a long time.

Veggies nearly done, add in the left over sausage meat. I had about 3/4 lb of meat to drop in. Fry all this together 'till the meat is cooked through.

Now dump back in the beans, the reserved pork from the bones and the bone broth.

Get this all boiling nicely. Time to adjust flavors. Test a spoonful and see if salt helps. Of so, add som in. Keep track of this though. DOn't overdo the salt. It's also going to need some sort of sugar. I put in about 1/4 cup of bottled BBQ sauce. Not enough to make the flavor obvious, just enough to push the flavor a little way in that direction. I finished the adjustments by adding a couple handfuls of sugar.

It turned out kinda' soupy. As was the plan.

This silly dish took most of the day to make. I only documented it because it turned out so amazingly good. We had it that evening. Then, by the end of the next day? It was gone!

I still have a lot of sausages to eat.
